WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.910
The energy lesson becomes safer when the learner stops asking where the energy went every time the oscillator changes position.

2
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:11.820
In ideal SHM, the total energy stays fixed.

3
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:17.730
What changes is the split between elastic potential energy and kinetic energy.

4
00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:23.640
The amplitude sets the total.

5
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:29.550
The chosen displacement sets how much of that total is stored elastically at that moment.

6
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:35.460
Whatever remains must then be kinetic.

7
00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:41.370
Once the learner trusts that ledger, speed questions become much easier because they are just another way of reading the kinetic share.

8
00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:47.280
Keep this takeaway in view: The energy lesson opens cleanly once the learner fixes the total first and treats the position as a split of that total.

9
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:53.190
Amplitude means sets the total.

10
00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:59.100
The total energy of the ideal mass-spring oscillator is fixed by the amplitude.

11
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:05.010
Displacement means sets the split.

12
00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:10.920
A chosen position decides how much energy is elastic and how much remains kinetic.

13
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.825
This diagram matters because it makes the energy story visual.

14
00:01:16.825 --> 00:01:22.730
Near a turning point, the elastic share is large and the kinetic share is tiny.

15
00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:28.635
Near equilibrium, the elastic share shrinks and the kinetic share grows.

16
00:01:28.635 --> 00:01:34.540
But the total bar remains fixed in the ideal model.

17
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:40.445
That is the key message.

18
00:01:40.445 --> 00:01:46.350
The oscillator is not creating or destroying energy as it moves.

19
00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:52.255
It is handing energy from one form to the other while keeping the total secure.

20
00:01:52.255 --> 00:01:58.160
Keep this takeaway in view: The energy board works because it shows a fixed total with a changing split instead of a disappearing-energy story.

21
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:04.065
Turning point: Maximum displacement means the elastic share is largest and the speed is zero.

22
00:02:04.065 --> 00:02:09.970
Equilibrium: Zero displacement means the elastic share is smallest and the speed is largest.

23
00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:14.807
A useful analogy is a budget shared between two wallets.

24
00:02:14.807 --> 00:02:19.643
One wallet stands for stored spring energy and the other stands for motion energy.

25
00:02:19.643 --> 00:02:24.480
At the outer edge, most of the money sits in the spring wallet.

26
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.317
At the center, most of it sits in the motion wallet.

27
00:02:29.317 --> 00:02:34.153
But the total budget does not disappear when the money moves between wallets.

28
00:02:34.153 --> 00:02:38.990
That is exactly the kind of thinking the learner needs in SHM.

29
00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:43.827
The form changes, but the total in the ideal model stays fixed.

30
00:02:43.827 --> 00:02:48.663
Keep this takeaway in view: The two-wallet analogy helps because it makes the total-versus-split distinction easy to remember.

31
00:02:48.663 --> 00:02:53.500
Spring wallet means stored stretch.

32
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:58.337
The elastic wallet grows when the oscillator is far from equilibrium.

33
00:02:58.337 --> 00:03:03.173
Motion wallet means kinetic share.

34
00:03:03.173 --> 00:03:08.010
The motion wallet grows when the oscillator passes through the center quickly.

35
00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:13.621
The energy equations are very structured here.

36
00:03:13.621 --> 00:03:19.231
Total energy is one half k A squared.

37
00:03:19.231 --> 00:03:24.842
Elastic potential energy at a chosen displacement is one half k x squared.

38
00:03:24.842 --> 00:03:30.453
Kinetic energy is then the total minus the elastic share.

39
00:03:30.453 --> 00:03:36.064
If speed is needed, kinetic energy equals one half m v squared.

40
00:03:36.064 --> 00:03:41.674
These equations matter because they create a clean order.

41
00:03:41.674 --> 00:03:47.285
Total first, chosen position second, subtraction third, speed last.

42
00:03:47.285 --> 00:03:52.896
That order stops the learner from hunting for speed before the ledger is complete.

43
00:03:52.896 --> 00:03:58.506
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene works because it forces the learner to complete the energy ledger before asking for speed.

44
00:03:58.506 --> 00:04:04.117
Amplitude is powerful: As soon as the amplitude is known, the total energy of the ideal oscillator is known.

45
00:04:04.117 --> 00:04:09.728
Do not skip subtraction: The kinetic share usually comes from the ledger difference, not from guesswork.

46
00:04:09.728 --> 00:04:15.339
E total = 1/2 kA^2.

47
00:04:15.339 --> 00:04:20.949
E elastic = 1/2 kx^2.

48
00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:26.560
The safest route is to secure the total energy first and only then discuss how it is split at a chosen position.

49
00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:33.788
A strong energy routine is to calculate the total from the amplitude first, calculate the elastic share from the chosen displacement second, subtract to get the kinetic share third, and convert kinetic energy into speed last if needed.

50
00:04:33.788 --> 00:04:41.016
This order matters because amplitude answers a different question from displacement.

51
00:04:41.016 --> 00:04:48.243
One sets the full energy budget of the oscillator, while the other tells you the current split.

52
00:04:48.243 --> 00:04:55.471
Keeping those jobs separate makes the later algebra much cleaner.

53
00:04:55.471 --> 00:05:02.699
Keep this takeaway in view: Total-split-speed is the safest SHM-energy routine because it separates the amplitude role from the displacement role.

54
00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:09.927
Step 1 means total from amplitude.

55
00:05:09.927 --> 00:05:17.154
The amplitude fixes the energy budget before the oscillator is placed at a specific position.

56
00:05:17.154 --> 00:05:24.382
Step 2 means split from displacement.

57
00:05:24.382 --> 00:05:31.610
The chosen x value tells you how much of the budget is currently elastic.

58
00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:38.523
The equilibrium snapshot matters because it reveals where the speed peaks.

59
00:05:38.523 --> 00:05:45.435
At the center, the displacement is zero, so the elastic potential energy is at its minimum.

60
00:05:45.435 --> 00:05:52.348
In the ideal mass-spring model, that means the full conserved total is available as kinetic energy there.

61
00:05:52.348 --> 00:05:59.260
At a turning point, the opposite is true: speed falls to zero while the elastic share is largest.

62
00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:06.172
Learners who keep that contrast clear rarely lose the maximum-speed mark in the worked example.

63
00:06:06.172 --> 00:06:13.085
Keep this takeaway in view: Center-versus-edge snapshots make maximum-speed reasoning obvious instead of memorized.

64
00:06:13.085 --> 00:06:19.998
Center: Zero displacement means the kinetic share is largest and the speed is maximum.

65
00:06:19.998 --> 00:06:26.910
Edge: Maximum displacement means the kinetic share is zero and the speed is zero.

66
00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:31.080
Try four checks.

67
00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.250
Does the amplitude fix the total energy in the ideal model?

68
00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:39.420
Yes.

69
00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:43.590
At a chosen displacement, can the elastic share be found before the kinetic share?

70
00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:47.760
Yes.

71
00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.930
Is the speed maximum at a turning point?

72
00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:56.100
No, it is maximum at equilibrium.

73
00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:00.270
And after the elastic share is known, should the kinetic share come from subtraction from the total?

74
00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:04.440
Yes.

75
00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.610
Those checks show whether the learner is running an energy ledger rather than inventing isolated formulas.

76
00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:12.780
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can defend total energy, split energy, and maximum-speed logic in one chain.

77
00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:16.950
Amplitude means sets the budget.

78
00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:21.120
Use the amplitude to lock in the total energy first.

79
00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.290
Chosen x means sets the split.

80
00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:29.460
Displacement tells you how the fixed budget is divided at that instant.

81
00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:36.115
You are ready for the worked example now.

82
00:07:36.115 --> 00:07:42.770
Start by using the amplitude to calculate the total energy of the ideal oscillator.

83
00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:49.425
Then use the chosen displacement to calculate the elastic share at that instant.

84
00:07:49.425 --> 00:07:56.080
Subtract to find the kinetic share.

85
00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:02.735
Finally, if speed is required, convert that kinetic share into a speed with the standard kinetic-energy relation.

86
00:08:02.735 --> 00:08:09.390
The strongest answers in this lesson sound like disciplined energy bookkeeping from the first line to the last.

87
00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:16.045
Keep this takeaway in view: The SHM-energy worked example becomes much safer once the learner treats amplitude as the budget setter and displacement as the split setter.

88
00:08:16.045 --> 00:08:22.700
Keep symbols separated: A and x play different roles in this lesson, so do not substitute one for the other.

89
00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:29.355
Use checkpoints: Center and edge snapshots are fast ways to test whether the answer sounds physically right.

90
00:08:29.355 --> 00:08:36.010
use the amplitude to fix E total first.

91
00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:42.665
use the chosen displacement to find the elastic share second.

92
00:08:42.665 --> 00:08:49.320
A good solution keeps the total energy visible all the way through the calculation.
